#: source/protocols/store-path.md:1
msgid "Complete Store Path Calculation"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:3
msgid ""
"This is the complete specification for how [store path](../store/store-path."
"md)s are calculated."
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:5
msgid ""
"The format of this specification is close to [Extended Backus–Naur form]"
"(https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extended_Backus%E2%80%93Naur_form), but must "
"deviate for a few things such as hash functions which we treat as "
"bidirectional for specification purposes."
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:7
msgid ""
"Regular users do _not_ need to know this information --- store paths can be "
"treated as black boxes computed from the properties of the store objects "
"they refer to. But for those interested in exactly how Nix works, e.g. if "
"they are reimplementing it, this information can be useful."
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:12
msgid "Store path proper"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:14
msgid ""
"```ebnf\n"
"store-path = store-dir \"/\" digest \"-\" name\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:17 source/protocols/store-path.md:100
msgid "where"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:19
msgid "`name` = the name of the store object."
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:21
msgid ""
"`store-dir` = the [path of the store directory](../store/store-path.md#store-"
"directory-path)"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:23
msgid ""
"`digest` = [Nix32](../protocols/nix32.md) representation of the compressed "
"to 160 bits [SHA-256](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/SHA-256) hash of "
"`fingerprint`."
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:25
msgid ""
"For the definition of the hash compression algorithm, please refer to "
"section 5.1 of the [Nix thesis](https://edolstra.github.io/pubs/phd-thesis."
"pdf)."
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:27
msgid "Fingerprint"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:29
msgid ""
"```ebnf\n"
"fingerprint = type \":sha256:\" inner-digest \":\" store \":\" name\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:33
msgid ""
"Note that it includes the location of the store as well as the name to make "
"sure that changes to either of those are reflected in the hash (e.g. you "
"won't get `/nix/store/<digest>-name1` and `/nix/store/<digest>-name2`, or `/"
"gnu/store/<digest>-name1`, with equal hash parts)."
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:36
msgid "`type` = one of:"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:38
msgid ""
"```ebnf\n"
"| \"text\" { \":\" store-path }\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:42
msgid ""
"This is for the [\"Text\"](../store/store-object/content-address.md#method-"
"text) method of content addressing store objects. The optional trailing "
"store paths are the references of the store object."
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:47
msgid ""
"```ebnf\n"
"| \"source\" { \":\" store-path } [ \":self\" ]\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:51
msgid ""
"This is for the [\"Nix Archive\"](../store/store-object/content-address."
"md#method-nix-archive) method of content addressing store objects, if the "
"hash algorithm is [SHA-256](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/SHA-256). Just "
"like in the \"Text\" case, we can have the store objects referenced by their "
"paths. Additionally, we can have an optional `:self` label to denote self-"
"reference."
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:58
msgid ""
"```ebnf\n"
"| \"output:\" id\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:62
msgid ""
"For either the outputs built from derivations, or content-addressed store "
"objects that are not using one of the two above cases. To be explicit about "
"the latter, that is currently these methods:"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:66
msgid "[\"Flat\"](../store/store-object/content-address.md#method-flat)"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:67
msgid "[\"Git\"](../store/store-object/content-address.md#method-git)"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:68
msgid ""
"[\"Nix Archive\"](../store/store-object/content-address.md#method-nix-"
"archive) if the hash algorithm is not [SHA-256](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/"
"wiki/SHA-256)."
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:70
msgid ""
"`id` is the name of the output (usually, \"out\"). For content-addressed "
"store objects, `id`, is always \"out\"."
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:73
msgid ""
"`inner-digest` = base-16 representation of a SHA-256 hash of `inner-"
"fingerprint`. The base-16 encoding uses lower-cased hex digits."
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:76
msgid "Inner fingerprint"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:78
msgid "`inner-fingerprint` = one of the following based on `type`:"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:80
msgid "if `type` = `\"text:\" ...`:"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:82
msgid "the string written to the resulting store path."
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:84
msgid "if `type` = `\"source:\" ...`:"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:86
msgid ""
"the [Nix Archive (NAR)](../store/file-system-object/content-address."
"md#serial-nix-archive) serialization of the [file system object](../store/"
"file-system-object.md) of the store object."
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:88
msgid "if `type` = `\"output:\" id`:"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:90
msgid "For input-addressed derivation outputs:"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:92
msgid ""
"the [ATerm](../protocols/derivation-aterm.md#input-address-encoding) "
"serialization of the [\"fully masked derivation\"](../store/derivation/"
"outputs/input-address.md#input-masked-drv), in which fixed-output inputs are "
"replaced by their content addresses and the derivation's own output paths "
"are blanked."
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:94
msgid "For content-addressed store paths:"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:96
msgid ""
"```ebnf\n"
"\"fixed:out:\" rec algo \":\" hash \":\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:102
msgid "`rec` = one of:"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:104
msgid ""
"```ebnf\n"
"| \"\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:107
msgid "(empty string) for hashes of the flat (single file) serialization"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:109
msgid ""
"```ebnf\n"
"| \"r:\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:112
msgid ""
"hashes of the for [Nix Archive (NAR)](../store/file-system-object/content-"
"address.md#serial-nix-archive) (arbitrary file system object) serialization"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:114
msgid ""
"```ebnf\n"
"| \"git:\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:117
msgid ""
"hashes of the [Git blob/tree](https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Internals-"
"Git-Objects) [Merkel tree](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Merkle_tree) format"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:119
msgid ""
"```ebnf\n"
"algo = \"md5\" | \"sha1\" | \"sha256\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:123
msgid ""
"`hash` = base-16 representation of the path or flat hash of the contents of "
"the path (or expected contents of the path for fixed-output derivations)."
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:125
msgid ""
"Note that `id` = `\"out\"`, regardless of the name part of the store path. "
"Also note that NAR + SHA-256 must not use this case, and instead must use "
"the `type` = `\"source:\" ...` case."
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:131
msgid "Historical Note"
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:133
msgid ""
"The `type` = `\"source:\" ...` and `type` = `\"output:out\"` grammars "
"technically overlap in purpose, in that both can represent data hashed by "
"its SHA-256 NAR serialization."
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:136
msgid ""
"The original reason for this way of computing names was to prevent name "
"collisions (for security). For instance, the thinking was that it shouldn't "
"be feasible to come up with a derivation whose output path collides with the "
"path for a copied source. The former would have an `inner-fingerprint` "
"starting with `output:out:`, while the latter would have an `inner-"
"fingerprint` starting with `source:`."
msgstr ""

#: source/protocols/store-path.md:140
msgid ""
"Since `64519cfd657d024ae6e2bb74cb21ad21b886fd2a` (2008), however, it was "
"decided that separating derivation-produced vs manually-hashed content-"
"addressed data like this was not useful. Now, data that is content-addressed "
"with SHA-256 + NAR-serialization always uses the `source:...` construction, "
"regardless of how it was produced (manually or by derivation). This allows "
"freely switching between using [fixed-output derivations](../glossary."
"md#gloss-fixed-output-derivation) for fetching, and fetching out-of-band and "
"then manually adding. It also removes the ambiguity from the grammar."
msgstr ""
